<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">24 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 24 May, from 919 to 1941.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/919">919 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,107 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Frankish and Saxon nobles elect Henry the Fowler king at Fritzlar</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a>, the nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> to take the throne. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> became emperor.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1487">1487 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">539 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The pretender Lambert Simnel is crowned Edward VI in Dublin</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The boy pretender Lambert Simnel, who claimed to be Edward Plantagenet, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a>, was crowned Edward VI in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christ-church-cathedral-dublin">Christ Church Cathedral, Dublin</a>. The Yorkist rebellion against King Henry VII that he fronted was crushed that year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry VII of England</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christ-church-cathedral-dublin">Christ Church Cathedral, Dublin</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1543">1543 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">483 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nicolaus Copernicus dies in Frombork</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Copernicus died weeks after his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres</a> was printed; tradition says he saw a copy on his deathbed. His Sun-centred model began a revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1689">1689 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">337 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Toleration Act grants freedom of worship to Dissenters</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Act allowed Protestant Nonconformists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to worship in their own licensed meeting houses. Catholics and anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinitarians">Trinitarians</a> were excluded.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1738">1738 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">288 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">John Wesley feels his heart 'strangely warmed'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At a meeting in Aldersgate Street, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, John Wesley experienced an assurance of faith while hearing Luther's preface to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> read. The experience energised the Methodist movement.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1844">1844 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">182 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Samuel Morse sends 'What hath God wrought'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Morse tapped out the biblical phrase from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> in Washington to Alfred Vail in Baltimore. The line opened the age of the electric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1883">1883 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">143 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Brooklyn Bridge opens across the East River</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After fourteen years of construction, the bridge linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> and Brooklyn opened. It was then the longest suspension bridge in the world, the first to use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> wire cables.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1941">1941 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">85 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bismarck sinks HMS Hood in the Denmark Strait</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A shell from the German battleship detonated Hood's magazine, and she sank in minutes; only three of her 1,418 crew survived. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> hunted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-battleship">Bismarck</a> down three days later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 24 May <span class="n">2</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/queen-victoria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Queen Victoria</span><span class="tp">1819–1901</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jan-smuts.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jan Smuts</span><span class="tp">1870–1950</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 24 May <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hasan-ali-ibn-othman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-al-hasan-ali-ibn-othman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Othman</span><span class="tp">d. 1351</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/taejo-of-joseon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Taejo of Joseon</span><span class="tp">1335–1408</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nicolaus-copernicus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Nicolaus Copernicus</span><span class="tp">1473–1543</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/george-brydges-rodney-1st-baron-rodney"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/george-brydges-rodney-1st-baron-rodney.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">George Brydges Rodney, 1st Baron Rodney</span><span class="tp">1718–1792</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/harold-wilson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Harold Wilson</span><span class="tp">1916–1995</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
